Cyber Lab Setup - Day 1: SIEM Infrastructure & Target Fleet Deployment
This repository documents the step-by-step building, configuring, and hardening of a dual-track cybersecurity laboratory. Day 1 focuses on deploying a centralized defensive monitoring architecture alongside an intentionally vulnerable testing application fleet using containerization.

🏗️ Lab Architecture Overview
The lab environment operates on a dedicated Ubuntu virtual machine managed within a hypervisor sandbox. The system infrastructure isolates defensive threat hunting analytics from target exploit frameworks using a fully dockerized stack.

The environment runs six concurrent containers split into two operational tracks:

1. Defensive Track (Wazuh SIEM Stack)
Wazuh Indexer: A highly performant data storage and search engine built on OpenSearch, responsible for compiling system event logs and generating searchable security analytics indices.

Wazuh Manager: The processing brain of the SIEM. It decodes system data, flags malicious activities against real-world compliance maps, and triggers alarms.

Wazuh Dashboard: The browser-based interface used for threat hunting, real-time alert parsing, and visualization mapping.

2. Offensive Track (Vulnerable Target Fleet)
Damn Vulnerable Web Application (DVWA): A PHP/MySQL-based application engineered to test security assessment tools and practice binary manual exploit patterns.

OWASP Juice Shop: A modern Node.js/Express web application filled with advanced security flaws across the entire OWASP Top 10 matrix.

WebGoat: A heavy Java-based training environment designed to explicitly test web application security vulnerabilities, injection flags, and structural application errors.

🚀 Step-by-Step Implementation
Step 1: SIEM Setup & Cryptographic Initialization
The deployment began by cloning the production Wazuh orchestration structure and dynamically generating localized internal TLS certificates to secure communication channels between the container vectors:

Bash
# Clone the orchestration repository version
git clone https://github.com/wazuh/wazuh-docker.git -b v4.12.0
cd wazuh-docker/single-node

# Run the single-use TLS certificate generator wrapper
docker compose -f generate-indexer-certs.yml run --rm generator
Step 2: Micro-Sizing Engine Variables
To adapt an enterprise-grade SIEM platform safely inside a localized, memory-capped development environment, optimization settings were adjusted in the underlying container definitions file (docker-compose.yml). The Java Options runtime heaps for the database indexing clusters were explicitly bounded to mitigate server crash sequences:

YAML
# Optimization inside wazuh.indexer -> environment configuration:
OPENSEARCH_JAVA_OPTS: "-Xms1g -Xmx1g"
Step 3: Spinning Up the Grid
Once configuration bounds were mapped out, the core architectural components were pulled down and initiated in background-daemon mode:

Bash
docker compose up -d
Step 4: Initializing the Offense Target Vectors
Standalone applications were provisioned using decoupled docker runtime engines to keep development structures fully separated:

Bash
# Deploy DVWA target environment
docker run -d -p 8080:80 --name dvwa vulnerables/web-dvwa

# Deploy OWASP Juice Shop target environment
docker run -d -p 3000:3000 --name juiceshop bkimminich/juice-shop

# Deploy WebGoat application frameworks and proxy configurations
docker run -d -p 8081:8080 -p 9090:9090 --name webgoat webgoat/webgoat
🚧 Roadblocks & Workarounds
Building a multi-vector cybersecurity lab on localized infrastructure presents standard virtualization conflicts. Below are the specific barriers encountered during deployment and the system engineering patterns applied to mitigate them:

1. OpenSearch Out-Of-Memory / Indexer Crash Loops
The Barrier: OpenSearch relies heavily on thread mappings that exceed default Linux kernel allocation limitations. On initialization, the container engine failed to map out indexing allocations and went into an immediate crash-restart loop.

The Workaround: Adjusted the system's global virtual memory mapping allocation tables straight via kernel parameters to grant the engine the baseline infrastructure runway it expected:

Bash
sudo sysctl -w vm.max_map_count=262144
2. Physical File System Exhaustion (no space left on device)
The Barrier: Pulling multiple massive security analytics images and application database containers completely choked the default 18 GB Logical Volume (LVM) partition allocation layout, resulting in a sudden, absolute termination of all installation routines.

The Workaround: Executed a systematic hypervisor storage expansion routine without losing localized environment configurations:

Cleanly powered down the local Linux server kernel via sudo poweroff.

Altered hardware constraints in the hypervisor settings panel to expand the virtual block layer allocation pool from 20 GB to 40 GB.

Re-authenticated to the instance and forced the system to aggressively parse, scale, and map out the newly provisioned block regions manually down through the device system tree using LVM mechanics:

Bash
# Step A: Expand block level hardware partition structure
sudo growpart /dev/sda 3

# Step B: Rescale physical volume boundaries inside LVM wrappers
sudo pvresize /dev/sda3

# Step C: Extend the Logical Volume to capture 100% of the new raw block space
sudo lvextend -l +100%FREE /dev/mapper/ubuntu--vg-ubuntu--lv

# Step D: Hot-resize the underlying ext4 system architecture on the fly
sudo resize2fs /dev/mapper/ubuntu--vg-ubuntu--lv
This successfully scaled the server workspace allocation directly down to 44% utilization, recovering 23 GB of pure free storage canvas to handle the heavy logs generated during lab operations.

3. Image Registry Call Denials (Upstream Documentation Typos)
The Barrier: Standard open-source platform documentation often contains stale naming conventions. Running raw configuration files matching bkimminich/juiceshop generated registry authentication failure loops.

The Workaround: Manually identified the official repository naming taxonomy trends on the global container registry platform and corrected the string parsing variables to access the hyphenated target directly: bkimminich/juice-shop.

